          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a common form of arthritis. It is a degenerative joint disease, which means it results from wear and tear on the joints over time. Osteoarthritis typically affects weight-bearing joints like the knees, hips, and spine. It involves the breakdown of cartilage, the tissue that cushions the ends of bones in joints. As the cartilage deteriorates, bones may rub against each other, leading to pain, stiffness, and loss of movement.\n\nIn contrast, the term \"arthritis\" refers to a broader category of disorders that cause inflammation in the joints. There are many types of arthritis, including rheumatoid arthritis, which is an autoimmune condition that causes chronic inflammation and can affect multiple joints symmetrically and involve other systems in the body.\n\nThe main difference between osteoarthritis and other forms of arthritis is that osteoarthritis is primarily related to mechanical wear and tear, while other forms of arthritis may involve systemic inflammation and immune system dysregulation. \n\nFor more details, the information can be found in the document titled \"Osteoarthritis\" from the general clinical reference.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
